      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Start with the pest services you want to grow</h2><p>Pest management businesses often offer several services: general household pest treatment, termite inspections, rodent management, mosquito control and commercial pest programs. Each attracts different customers and may involve different inspection or booking requirements.</p><p>Prioritize the services your technicians are properly licensed and equipped to deliver. Check coverage area, seasonal capacity, service availability and profitability before spending money to attract additional inquiries.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Capture customers already searching for help</h2><p>Google Ads can reach local customers actively searching for an exterminator, pest control company or specific service. Match advertisements to the exact service rather than directing all inquiries to a generic homepage.</p><p>Organic search can support those services over time. Clear service pages, accurate business information, genuine reviews and locally useful content help visitors determine whether the company can solve their problem.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Separate inspections from routine service</h2><p>Termite concerns may require an inspection before a contractor can recommend treatment. A recurring pest-maintenance customer may need information about coverage, scheduling and what the plan includes. A commercial property manager may require a site assessment and a longer approval process.</p><p>Build service-specific funnels around these differences. Never promise that a short marketing form can confirm a pest species, infestation severity or treatment method.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Build mobile-friendly lead capture</h2><p>Give customers a visible phone option and a short form that asks for their location, property type, pest concern and preferred contact time. Explain whether they are requesting an inspection, a consultation or a service appointment.</p><p>Avoid asking visitors to touch pests, use chemicals or investigate unsafe areas to qualify for a quote. Pest identification and treatment decisions belong with trained professionals.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Route and follow up with new inquiries</h2><p>Use a CRM or field-service system to record the source, service requested, assigned staff member and contact outcome. Give urgent service requests and planned commercial consultations an appropriate handoff process.</p><p>Administrative reminders can help prevent missed inquiries, but the business should respect communication preferences and relevant consent requirements. A response process only works when someone owns the next action.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure booked work instead of only lead volume</h2><p>Track advertising inquiries, qualified contacts, confirmed inspections, service bookings and completed jobs as different outcomes. Booking-button clicks are not confirmed appointments.</p><p>Review which services generate suitable work and whether the cost of acquiring those customers fits the economics of the initial treatment and any legitimate recurring-service relationship.</p></section>

